critic.formats:
These formats already match the real exam:
- penalty-scored multiple choice (te2-02 K1, te2-10 K1; the same 2/−1/0 scheme as autumn 2025 task 1 and autumn 2026 task 1);
- naming concepts (te2-03, 05 and 09 K1; spring 2025 task 1);
- error finding (te2-07 K1; autumn 2026 task 4, spring 2026 task 1);
- table completion (te2-04 K2, te2-07 K2; autumn 2026 task 3);
- applying the five Ottawa areas (te2-04, 05 and 10 K3; autumn 2023 task 8.2, spring 2025 task 7.2);
- research plan (te2-02 K3; spring 2026 task 9);
- AI ethics (te2-03 K2; spring 2025 task 9);
- define-and-exemplify (te2-01 K1; autumn 2023 task 2);
- drawing a model (te2-09 K2);
- one bar chart (te2-08 K2a).

These are missing or weaker than the real exam:
1. A 20-item true/false set (spring 2024 task 1, autumn 2023 tasks 1 and 4).
2. List-and-count with one point per correct item (spring 2023 task 3).
3. Real data media: a risk map (spring 2024 task 4), product labels and pictures (autumn 2024 task 7, spring 2025 task 4), and video (autumn 2023 task 9, autumn 2026 task 7). Our material is almost all fictional text and small tables.
4. Two-series charts pasted as screenshots, with findings scored one point each (spring 2025 task 7.1).
5. Character limits with overage deductions (autumn 2023 task 2: 900 characters, −1 or −2) and the 3-5 point deduction for not using the material (autumn 2023 task 5, autumn 2025 task 4). Our model answers do not state these.
6. Tiered content rubrics (3/6/9 points for 1/3/5 elements) next to a separate 0-10 information-processing score. Our Pisteytys splits by element, which is close but not the same.

Overall, the task types match well. The real gap is the material media and the scoring conventions.
